# Maintain a changelog

When a change is significant, update `CHANGELOG.md` in the same commit.

## Structure

- Keep entries under `## Unreleased` until a release is tagged.
- Use these categories in this exact order:
  - `### Breaking changes`
  - `### Features`
  - `### Fixes`
- On release, rename `## Unreleased` to `## <version> - <date>` and add a new
  empty `## Unreleased` section at the top.

## Entry format

Group entries under `####` headings. Each bullet is
`- <lowercase user-facing summary>` with no component prefix.

- Components: `iosapp`, `webapp`, `server`, `caserver`, `gitdb`,
  `git-replycant`, `replycant-importer`.
- Heading map: `iosapp` → `iOS app`; `webapp` → `Desktop app`;
  `server` / `caserver` → `Server`; `git-replycant` and
  `replycant-importer` keep their slugs.
- Group by the product the user notices, not the implementing
  component. `gitdb` is shared: use `Server` only for hosted-stack
  changes; otherwise put it under the client or as a cross-cutting
  bullet.
- Emit a heading only when it has at least one entry.
- Cross-cutting bullets go first, with no heading.
- Then headings in this order: `iOS app`, `Desktop app`, `Server`,
  `git-replycant`, `replycant-importer`.

```markdown
### Fixes

- add regenerable product screenshots in the README

#### iOS app

- photo uploads now refuse an unsupported `gitdb/version`

#### replycant-importer

- failed commits no longer leave staged-but-deleted files
```

Do not write `- iosapp: ...` or any other `component:` prefix.

## One entry per feature

- A feature is one entry, however many commits it took to build. When a later
  commit in the same branch refines the feature, update the existing entry
  instead of adding another one.
- Fold copy tweaks, UI polish, and bugs found while building the feature into
  that entry. Users never saw the intermediate states, so they are not fixes.
- Keep a separate entry only when the change stands on its own: it changes
  behavior outside the new feature, or it matters to someone who never uses
  the feature.

## What to include

- Include user-visible behavior changes, new capabilities, breaking
  API/schema/protocol/data format changes, security fixes, and notable bug
  fixes.
- Skip refactors, test-only changes, docs, CI tweaks, formatting, and internal
  cleanups with no observable effect.

## Writing guidance

- Write for users, not reviewers.
- Describe impact and outcomes, not implementation details.
- For breaking changes, state what breaks and what users must do next. In alpha
  stage, wiping state is an acceptable instruction.
